## 6. System Architecture Design

The logical architecture for Retail-X-AI is structured as a layered design that separates responsibilities and encourages clarity, maintainability, and testability.

### 6.1 Architecture layers

| Layer | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Presentation Layer | Displays dashboards, alerts, recommendations, and AI assistant responses |
| Application Layer | Validates requests, coordinates operations, and enforces authorisation |
| Domain Services Layer | Implements sales, inventory, forecasting, risk assessment, and recommendation logic |
| AI / ML Layer | Produces forecasts and supports AI-grounded assistance |
| Data Layer | Stores logical product, sales, forecast, and recommendation data |

### 6.2 Core design principles

The architecture follows these principles:

- separation of concerns
- clear data ownership
- validation of input data
- transparent forecasting assumptions
- no automatic decision-making without human review
- support for explainable outputs
- secure handling of user and business information

### 6.3 Architectural assumptions

The current design assumes:

- the system will remain a decision-support platform rather than an autonomous purchasing system
- the historical dataset remains a key data source
- product, inventory, and sales records are the foundation for forecasting and risk detection
- AI assistance must be grounded in approved system data
- the shop owner or manager retains final responsibility for business actions

